    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "explanation": "The fault described is an over-long front-to-back split with the front knee collapsing inward on the catch, so the direct correction is shortening the fore-aft split distance and cueing a vertical shin with the knee tracking over the toe, which restores a stable, aligned landing position. Option A addresses side-to-side width, but the fault described is in the sagittal (front-to-back) plane and depth of the split, not lateral stability, so widening the stance side-to-side does not correct an over-long fore-aft split or the resulting valgus. Option C changes the timing of the drive relative to the split, but the problem is the landing position itself, not that the athlete lacks time to find it, so driving the bar higher first does not correct where or how the feet land."
